course: french
lesson: 6
title: Numbers 0 to 20
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Counting to twenty in French is like learning a new playground chant: un, deux, trois, quatre, cinq, and soon your mouth does it on its own.
explain: Several numbers hide silent letters: sept sounds like "set" because the p is asleep, and vingt sounds like "van" because the g and the t are asleep.
explain: Eleven to sixteen each have their own word ending in -ze (onze, douze, treize, quatorze, quinze, seize), a bit like English eleven and twelve, which don't say "one-one".
explain: From seventeen French builds the number: dix-sept is ten-seven, dix-huit ten-eight and dix-neuf ten-nine, joined with a hyphen.
explain: Picture a bakery in Lyon: hold up your thumb and pointing finger and the baker gives you two croissants, because the French start counting on the thumb.
fact: 1 to 5: un ("uhn"), deux ("duh"), trois ("trwa"), quatre ("katr"), cinq ("sank", said through the nose with a k at the end).
fact: 6 to 10: six ("seess"), sept ("set", the p is silent), huit ("weet", the h is silent), neuf ("nuhf"), dix ("deess").
fact: The x of six and dix changes: "ss" on its own, silent before a consonant (six pommes, "see POM"), and "z" before a vowel (six amis, "see-za-MEE").
fact: In France people usually start counting on the thumb: thumb is one, thumb and pointing finger are two.
fact: 11 to 16: onze ("onz"), douze ("dooz"), treize ("trez"), quatorze ("ka-TORZ"), quinze ("kanz"), seize ("sez").
fact: 17 to 19 are built with a hyphen: dix-sept ("deess-SET"), dix-huit ("deez-WEET"), dix-neuf ("deez-NUHF"); in dix-huit and dix-neuf the x sounds like z.
fact: vingt means 20 and sounds like "van", through the nose; the g and t are silent letters left over from the old Latin word viginti.
fact: zéro means 0 and sounds like "zay-RO", because é says "ay".
fact: "One" has two forms: un for masculine words and une for feminine words, so it is un livre (a book) but une baguette.
fact: Jacques a dit ("zhak a DEE") is the French Simon says; sautez dix fois means "jump ten times".
fact: La marelle ("la ma-REL") is hopscotch, and children often call out the number of each square as they land.
fact: Slang here: "lowkey" means a bit; "pure mental" (Glasgow) means totally mad; "yeah nah" (Australia, New Zealand) means no; "salty" means annoyed; "tidy" (Wales) means good; "butt" (Wales) means mate; "wicked" (Maine) means very.
exercise: Jacob counts to five. Which is cinq? | "sank", through the nose with a k at the end | "sink", like in a kitchen | "sin-kew", with two parts | "sank-uh", with an extra sound
exercise: Which number has a silent p? | sept (7) | six (6) | dix (10) | neuf (9)
exercise: How does huit (8) sound? | "weet" | "hoot", like an owl | "hweet", with a puff | "wit"
exercise: The market man says six pommes. How does six sound here? | "see", with the x silent | "seess", with a clear s | "seeks", with a k | "seez", with a z
exercise: How do you say six amis (six friends)? | "see-za-MEE" | "seess a-MEE" | "see a-MEE" | "seeks a-MEE"
exercise: How do French people usually show two with their fingers? | Thumb and pointing finger | Pointing finger and middle finger | Two thumbs up | Little finger and ring finger
exercise: What is 11 in French? | onze | dix-un | douze | un-un
exercise: Which group is the -ze family from 11 to 16? | onze, douze, treize, quatorze, quinze, seize | onze, douze, treize, dix-quatre, quinze, seize | onze, deuze, troize, quatorze, cinqze, seize | dix-un, dix-deux, dix-trois, dix-quatre, dix-cinq, dix-six
exercise: What does dix-huit mean? | 18 | 8 | 10 | 108
exercise: How does dix-huit sound? | "deez-WEET" | "dicks-HOOT" | "dee-HWEET" | "deess-hoo-EET"
exercise: How do you say vingt (20)? | "van", through the nose | "vingt-ee" | "ving" | "vint", like a mint
exercise: How do you say zéro? | "zay-RO" | "ZEE-ro" | "ZAIR-oh" | "zuh-ROO"
exercise: Max asks for "un baguette". What should he say? | une baguette | un baguettes | une baguet | uns baguette
exercise: In Jacques a dit, when must you do the action? | Only when the leader says "Jacques a dit" first | Every time the leader says a number | Only when the leader points at you | Only when the leader says "s'il vous plaît"
exercise: What is la marelle? | hopscotch | skipping | hide and seek | the sea
exercise: What is quinze? | 15 | 5 | 50 | 14
test: Jacob asks, "Is five really 'sank' like a ship?" What's the answer? | Yes, cinq sounds like "sank", said through the nose | No, it is "sink", like the kitchen sink | No, it is "sin-kew", with two parts | Yes, but the q is always fully silent
test: Olivia in Seattle counts "sicks, septic, hoot" and laughs, "Lowkey sounds like a sick owl." ("Lowkey" means a bit.) What's the fix? | "seess, set, weet" | "sicks, sept, hoot" | "see, sep, oot" | "seez, setic, hweet"
test: Imogen mutters, "Make your mind up, mate," when six sounds like "see" before pommes. Why does six change? | Its x is silent before a consonant, "ss" alone, "z" before a vowel | The man was saying a different number by mistake | Six is said "see" only when you talk about fruit | The man forgot to finish the word
test: Noah thinks the French boy counting from his thumb is "doing it wrong". Who is right? | The French boy: in France people usually start on the thumb | Noah: everyone in the whole world starts counting on the pointing finger | Noah: the thumb is only used for counting in maths | The French boy: starting on the pointing finger is rude
test: Lewis moans, "Pure mental, they don't even look like one-one, one-two." ("Pure mental" means totally mad.) What's the best reply? | 11 to 16 have their own words, like English eleven and twelve | He's right, so French people say dix-un and dix-deux | The French count only to ten, then use English | They are named after the months
test: Ava says dix-huit as "dicks-hoot" and the class roars. What should she say? | "deez-WEET" | "deez-HOOT" | "dicks-WEET" | "dee-WEE"
test: Arjun asks, "Sir, why's there a g and a t in vingt when it's just 'van'?" What's the truth? | The letters are left over from the old Latin word | French kids added them to make it longer | They are said very quietly, so you hear them | The g and t are a mistake in the dictionary
test: Skye's brother says, "Yeah nah, it's 'zay-ro', isn't it?" ("Yeah nah" means no.) Who's right about zéro? | The brother: é says "ay" | Skye: it's "ZEE-ro", as in English | Neither: it's "ZAIR-oh" | Both: it doesn't matter at all
test: Max whispers, "Bro, she corrected me!" when the baker says une baguette, and he is very salty. ("Salty" means annoyed.) Why did she? | Baguette is feminine, so "one" is une | Une is how you say "a bit of" bread | Un is only used for numbers above ten | The baker was just being cheeky
test: Ethan's mate in Cardiff laughs, "Tidy, but count, butt!" ("Tidy" means good; "butt" means mate.) He jumped 11 times for "sautez dix fois". How many too many? | 1 | 10 | 2 | 0
test: ★★★ A hopscotch grid has squares 1 to 10. Jessica lands on sept, then huit, then neuf. Which squares are left after neuf, and what is the French for it? | One square: dix | Two squares: dix and onze | One square: six | None: neuf is the last one
test: ★★★ Treize + cinq = ? (Give the French.) | dix-huit | dix-sept | quinze | dix-neuf
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the pronunciation of the numeral sept? | The p is silent, the word being pronounced as "set" | The p is pronounced with particular force | The terminal t is silent, the word being pronounced as "sep" | Both p and t are silent, the word being pronounced as "say"
exam: Identify the French numeral denoting the quantity twelve. | douze | deux | treize | dix-deux
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the number seventeen. | It is formed by joining dix and sept with a hyphen | It is a single word ending in -ze, namely septze | It is formed by joining sept and dix, as sept-dix | It is expressed as vingt minus trois
exam: It is given that the customary French manner of indicating numbers upon the fingers commences with a particular digit. Determine that digit. | The thumb | The index finger | The little finger | The middle finger
